Each `code` task runs in an isolated worktree under `.steiner/worktrees/`. Use the returned `worktree_path` and `worktree_branch` from delegation result to inspect or merge it. Check delegation warnings for unseen parent-tree changes. After merging and verification, always prune the worktree. No shared-tree fallback exists.

Run independent sub-agents together. Sequence only when a call depends on another's result or shares mutable state/files with it.

## Deliverable boundaries

A deliverable is one concrete, self-contained implementation outcome with a settled design and bounded write scope that one `code` sub-agent can own through implementation, fixes, and verification.

Each deliverable must materially advance the task. Do not split work into isolated mechanical edits.

Split to create independent ownership: parallel deliverables should have disjoint write sets and no unresolved cross-task contracts. Serialize only irreducibly shared mutations and assign one integration owner.

Mechanical propagation of a settled design may cross files or packages without becoming separate deliverables.

If affected files, ownership, or contracts are unclear, use `explore` first.

These boundaries govern task sizing, parallelism, and `follow_up` reuse.

## Continuing sub-agents

Prefer `follow_up` on a sub-agent when the session is resumable, the work stays within the same deliverable, and its live workspace still exists.

Use it for continuation or fixes within the worker's existing domain. For new domains or after workspace cleanup/handoff, dispatch a new sub-agent.

